---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import Button from '../../components/ui/Button.astro';
import CodeTabs, { type Tab } from '../../components/docs/CodeTabs.astro';
import Flow from '../../components/docs/Flow.astro';
import { SITE } from '../../data/site';
// One source for the connect snippets and the tool list: the dashboard's API keys page shows the same ones.
import { AGENT_TOOLS, agentSnippets } from '../../../dashboard/src/features/api-keys/snippets';

const MCP_URL = 'https://api.truplexy.com/v3/mcp';

const agentTabs: Tab[] = [
  ...agentSnippets(MCP_URL).map((s) => ({ id: s.id, label: s.label, files: [{ name: s.name, code: s.code }] })),
  {
    id: 'other',
    label: 'Other clients',
    files: [],
    html: `<p>Any MCP client that speaks <strong>Streamable HTTP</strong> works, the MCP Inspector included, as long as it can send a header. Point it at <code>${MCP_URL}</code> and send the key in the <code>Authorization: Bearer tpxa_…</code> header. The endpoint is stateless and answers in JSON, so no session or event stream is needed.</p>`,
  },
];

const toc = [
  ['overview', 'Two directions'],
  ['servers', 'Give your bot MCP tools'],
  ['agents', 'Let AI agents work your inbox'],
  ['agent-key', '1. Issue an agent key'],
  ['connect', '2. Connect the agent'],
  ['tools', 'What agents can do'],
  ['security', 'Keys and safety'],
] as const;
---

<Docs
  title="MCP and AI agents — Truplexy"
  breadcrumbs={[{ name: 'Docs', path: '/docs' }, { name: 'MCP and AI agents', path: '/docs/mcp' }]}
  description="Connect MCP servers so your Truplexy bot can call their tools, and let AI agents like Claude Code, Cursor and Claude Desktop work your support inbox over MCP with an agent key."
  eyebrow="Developers"
  heading="MCP and AI agents"
  lede="Truplexy speaks the Model Context Protocol both ways. Your bot can call the tools of any remote MCP server, and AI agents such as Claude Code, Cursor and Claude Desktop can read and answer your tickets through Truplexy’s own MCP endpoint. Both come with Pro and Enterprise."
  toc={toc}
>
  <Fragment slot="hero-actions">
    <Button href={`${SITE.dashboardUrl}/`}>Open the dashboard</Button>
    <Button href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io" variant="ghost" arrow={false}>What is MCP?</Button>
  </Fragment>

  <h2 id="overview">Two directions</h2>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>You want</th><th>Set it up in</th><th>Who calls whom</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Your bot to look things up or act in another system while it answers customers</td><td><strong>Tools → MCP servers</strong></td><td>Truplexy calls your MCP server</td></tr>
        <tr><td>An AI agent to read tickets, reply, search the knowledge base or pull stats</td><td><strong>API keys → Connect an AI agent</strong></td><td>The agent calls Truplexy</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="servers">Give your bot MCP tools</h2>
  <p>
    Many services publish their actions as a remote MCP server, and you can run your own in front of your systems. Add one, and its tools join the tool library next to the
    built-in ones. Your bot calls them while it replies, the same way it calls an API tool.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>In the <a href={SITE.dashboardUrl}>dashboard</a>, open <strong>Tools</strong> and choose <strong>Add MCP server</strong>.</li>
    <li>Give it a name, its <strong>Server URL</strong> (an <code>https://</code> Streamable HTTP endpoint) and a short <strong>Tool prefix</strong>.</li>
    <li>If the server needs a key, add one header, such as <code>Authorization</code> with <code>Bearer …</code> or <code>X-API-Key</code>. It is stored encrypted and never shown again.</li>
    <li>Truplexy connects, lists the server’s tools and saves the server. If it can’t connect, you see the server’s own error and nothing is saved.</li>
    <li>From the server’s menu, choose <strong>Let &lt;your bot&gt; use its tools</strong>, or tick tools one by one under the bot’s tools. Save the bot.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Tools are named <code>mcp_&lt;prefix&gt;_&lt;tool&gt;</code>, and their description starts with the server’s name, so you can tell where each came from. When the server adds
    or changes tools, choose <strong>Sync tools</strong>. A business can connect up to 10 servers, and a bot can use up to 20 tools in all.
  </p>
  <h3>What the bot can call</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Remote servers only.</strong> The URL must be public <code>https://</code>. Private, loopback and cloud metadata addresses are refused, and redirects are not followed.</li>
    <li><strong>No sign-in flows yet.</strong> Servers that need no key, or one fixed header, work today. Servers that only take an OAuth sign-in (such as some hosted SaaS servers) don’t, yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Simple inputs.</strong> A tool whose inputs use <code>anyOf</code>, <code>oneOf</code> or <code>$ref</code>, or nest too deeply, is listed as unavailable with the reason, never changed behind your back.</li>
    <li><strong>Each call has 10 seconds,</strong> like any tool, and every call is logged under the bot’s tool runs.</li>
    <li><strong>Downgrades turn them off.</strong> On a plan without MCP the tools show as unavailable and the bot skips them. Nothing is deleted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="agents">Let AI agents work your inbox</h2>
  <Flow
    description="An AI agent connects to Truplexy's MCP endpoint with an agent key, and works one bot's tickets, knowledge base and customers with the permissions of that key."
    nodes={[
      { label: 'Your agent', title: 'Claude, Cursor…', text: 'Any MCP client over Streamable HTTP.' },
      { label: 'Truplexy MCP', title: 'An agent key', text: 'Read only, or read & write. One bot.' },
      { label: 'Your inbox', title: 'Tickets & knowledge', text: 'Same rules as a team member.' },
    ]}
  />
  <p>
    Ask your agent to summarise what’s waiting, draft answers to the open tickets, find a customer’s history or add what it learned to the knowledge base. It works through
    the same endpoints as the dashboard, with their checks, webhooks and live updates, so your team sees its replies and changes as they happen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agent-key">1. Issue an agent key</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>In the <a href={SITE.dashboardUrl}>dashboard</a>, pick the bot, open <strong>API keys</strong> and choose <strong>Connect an AI agent</strong>.</li>
    <li>Name the key after the agent, such as <em>Claude</em>. Its name signs the replies it sends.</li>
    <li>Choose <strong>Read only</strong> to let it look, or <strong>Read &amp; write</strong> to let it reply and make changes.</li>
    <li>Copy the key, which starts <code>tpxa_</code>. It is shown once.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2 id="connect">2. Connect the agent</h2>
  <p>The endpoint is <code>{MCP_URL}</code>. After you issue a key, the dashboard shows these with your key filled in.</p>
  <CodeTabs group="agent" label="Your agent" tabs={agentTabs} />
  <p>Then ask it something like <em>“Which Truplexy tickets need a reply? Summarise them.”</em></p>

  <h2 id="tools">What agents can do</h2>
  <p>An agent sees only the tools its key allows. A read key has the first six; a write key has all of them.</p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Tool</th><th>Key</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {AGENT_TOOLS.map((t) => (
          <tr><td><code>{t.name}</code></td><td>{t.access === 'read' ? 'Read' : 'Write'}</td><td>{t.what}</td></tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="security">Keys and safety</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>One bot per key.</strong> An agent key reaches only the bot it was issued for.</li>
    <li><strong>A team member’s permissions, no more.</strong> A read key can do what a viewer can; a write key what a support agent can, plus adding knowledge. Neither reaches integrations, members or billing.</li>
    <li><strong>Separate from chat keys.</strong> Agent keys work only on the MCP endpoint, and chat keys never work there.</li>
    <li><strong>Accountable.</strong> Replies are signed with the key’s name, and the audit log records every change against the key.</li>
    <li><strong>Revoke any time</strong> under <strong>API keys</strong>. The agent loses access within a minute.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>For the raw protocol and the <code>POST /api-keys</code> fields, see the <a href="/docs/api">REST API</a>.</p>
</Docs>
